.TH VOP_WHITEOUT 9 "February 6, 2026" "Substrate" "Kernel Developer's Manual"
.SH NAME
VOP_WHITEOUT \- create, delete, or lookup a whiteout entry
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <vfs/vnode.h>
.B #include <sys/namei.h>
.PP
.BI "int VOP_WHITEOUT(struct vnode *dvp, struct componentname *cnp, int flags);"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B VOP_WHITEOUT()
function manipulates whiteout entries in a directory. Whiteouts are used by union filesystems to hide entries in lower layers.
.PP
The arguments are:
.TP
.I dvp
The vnode of the directory.
.TP
.I cnp
The component name information.
.TP
.I flags
One of
.B CREATE\fR,
.B DELETE\fR,
or
.B LOOKUP\fR.
.SH LOCKS
The directory
.I dvp
should be locked on entry and will remain locked on return.
.SH RETURN VALUES
Zero is returned on success, otherwise an error code is returned.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
[EOPNOTSUPP]
The filesystem does not support whiteouts.
.TP
[ENOTDIR]
The
.I dvp
argument is not a directory.
.TP
[EROFS]
The filesystem is read-only.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vnode (9),
.BR namei (9)
